---
// "wholesale burger boxes" drew 1,365 impressions and zero clicks at position
// 13 with nothing on the site addressing it. It is a different question from
// the product pages -- it is about ordering in bulk, not about a box -- so it
// gets its own page rather than another product variant.
import Shell from '../layouts/Shell.astro';
import PageBody from '../components/PageBody.astro';
import { authoredPage } from '../lib/authored.js';
import GuideList from '../components/GuideList.astro';
import { PROD_ORIGIN } from '../lib/site.js';

const page = authoredPage({
  route: '/wholesale-burger-boxes/',
  title: 'Wholesale Burger Boxes | The Burger Boxes',
  description: 'Buy burger boxes wholesale: from $0.20 a unit, minimum order 100, free design and free shipping. Custom sizes, materials and printing on every run.',
});

const schema = [{
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: PROD_ORIGIN + '/' },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Wholesale Burger Boxes', item: PROD_ORIGIN + '/wholesale-burger-boxes/' },
  ],
}];
---
<Shell page={page} schema={schema}>
  <PageBody postId={9002} title="Wholesale Burger Boxes" crumb="Wholesale Burger Boxes" route="/wholesale-burger-boxes/">
    <p>
      We supply burger boxes wholesale to restaurants, cafes, food trucks,
      takeaways, ghost kitchens and caterers across the country. Every run is
      printed to order rather than picked from stock, which means the size, the
      board, the print and the finish are yours to set rather than ours to
      offer. Prices start at $0.20 a unit, the minimum order is 100 units, and
      design and shipping are included in the quote.
    </p>

    <h2>What wholesale means here</h2>
    <p>
      There is no standing catalog of pre-made boxes waiting in a warehouse.
      You tell us the dimensions, the style and the quantity, and we print that
      run for you. That is why the figure on each product page is a starting
      point rather than a fixed price — the unit cost falls as the run gets
      longer, and the only honest way to a firm number is to quote against a
      real specification.
    </p>
    <p>
      It also means the minimum exists for a reason. Setting up a print run
      costs the same whether it produces a hundred boxes or a hundred thousand,
      so very short runs cost more per box than they are worth to either of us.
      One hundred units is where that stops being true.
    </p>

    <h2>What we need to price a run</h2>
    <p>
      Three things get you an accurate quote. First, the internal dimensions the
      box has to hold — measure your tallest regular build with the bun on, and
      give us length, width and height rather than a description. Second, the
      style and board. Third, the quantity, or a range if you are still
      deciding.
    </p>
    <p>
      Artwork is useful but not required to start. Our designers set the files
      up for you at no charge, so a logo and a rough idea of the look are enough
      for a first quote, and the artwork can be finalized before the run goes to
      press. If you are unsure of the size, describe the build and we will
      suggest one from the
      <a href="/product-category/boxes-by-size/">standard burger box sizes</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Choosing the specification</h2>
    <p>
      Board and style are the two decisions that move the price most, and they
      are also the two that decide whether the food survives the journey. Kraft,
      cardboard, corrugated, paper and bagasse each behave differently with heat
      and grease, and the trade-offs are set out under
      <a href="/product-category/boxes-by-material/">burger boxes by material</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Style decides how the box opens, how it stacks and how well it holds a
      tall build — a clamshell for counter service, a deeper parcel box for
      delivery, a window box where the product needs to show. The range is
      grouped under <a href="/product-category/boxes-by-type/">burger box types</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      For the wider background on how a specification comes together, and what
      each choice actually changes, see our guide to
      <a href="/burger-box-packaging/">specifying burger box packaging</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Printing and branding</h2>
    <p>
      Full-color printing is available on every line, with gloss, matte and spot
      UV finishes, and free design work to prepare the files. For a first order,
      plain colored stock in black, brown, kraft, pink, red or white is often
      the fastest route to something that looks deliberate, with printed
      artwork added on the reorder once the design has settled.
    </p>
    <p>
      Logo boxes, printed sleeves, branded meal boxes and stickers are grouped
      under <a href="/product-category/boxes-by-customization/">custom printed burger packaging</a>.
      Ordering fries boxes, cups, sauce pots and delivery bags on the same run
      keeps the artwork consistent across the whole order and usually costs less
      than sourcing them separately.
    </p>

    <h2>Reordering</h2>
    <p>
      Once a run has been printed we hold the specification and the artwork, so
      a repeat order is a quantity and a date rather than a fresh brief. That is
      also the point at which longer runs make sense: if the design has proven
      itself over a first order, extending the quantity is the cheapest change
      you can make to your unit price.
    </p>

    <h2>Start a wholesale order</h2>
    <p>
      Send the size, the style and the quantity and we will come back with
      wholesale pricing and a production timeline. Use the
      <a href="/contact-us/">wholesale quote form</a>, or call (503) 358-0443 and
      talk it through.
    </p>
    <GuideList heading="Guides" intro="Longer answers to the questions that come up before a specification is settled." />
  </PageBody>
</Shell>
